---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Omnipeak"
  description="Omnipeak is the engineering practice of David, a software engineer who closes the gap between business owners and developers by being both: the one who listens and the one who builds."
>
  <section class="section page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>The person who listens is the person who builds</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Omnipeak is the engineering practice of David, a software engineer based in Los Angeles
        and Orange County, working with small businesses across the US and completing a
        master's in computer science at Georgia Tech.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Why Omnipeak exists</h2>
        <p>
          There has always been a gap between business owners and the developers they hire. The
          owner knows exactly how the business runs but not how software gets built. The
          developer knows how to build but not why the business works the way it does. Somewhere
          in between, the important details get lost, and the owner ends up paying for software
          that doesn't quite fit.
        </p>
        <p>
          Larger firms try to fix that with people in the middle: a salesperson, a project
          manager, someone who translates. Every extra person is one more place for a detail to
          slip. Omnipeak closes the gap by being both sides of it. The person who sits down to
          understand your business is the same person who writes the code.
        </p>

        <h2>What that means for you</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Decisions happen in one conversation.</strong> Nothing waits on internal
            meetings or gets passed to a junior developer.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Answers in plain English.</strong> You hear what's happening and why, without
            the jargon.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>One person accountable.</strong> From the database your records live in, to
            the software, to keeping it running, there's no layer to hide behind when something
            needs fixing.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Deliberately boring technology</h2>
        <p>
          Omnipeak builds on proven, widely used technology (TypeScript, PostgreSQL, and Stripe),
          chosen precisely because tens of thousands of developers know it. That's your
          protection: if you ever want someone else to maintain your system, you'll be able to
          find them. Clever, obscure technology is a form of lock-in, and Omnipeak doesn't use
          it.
        </p>

        <h2>Why the hard problems are the useful ones</h2>
        <p>
          Much of the work so far has been reservation and billing systems. They look simple and
          aren't: time-slot logic, recurring payments, failed charges, memberships that pause and
          resume. This is exactly where off-the-shelf tools run out and where half-finished
          custom projects tend to die.
        </p>
        <p>
          Software that has to be right about money and time, every day, without someone
          watching it, sets the standard for everything else. The question worth asking isn't
          whether your industry is on a list. It's whether the thing you need built can be
          explained clearly, and that's what the first call is for.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTA />
</BaseLayout>
